      {
        "id": "p11-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Calculate the gradient of the graph.\nWhat is its meaning in this context?\n(Hint: think of the simple interest formula)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Gradient: $50$ dollars of interest per $1000$ dollars of principal. This is $0.05$ dollar per dollar, an annual interest rate of $5\\%$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}}\\\\m&=\\frac{500-100}{10-2}\\\\m&=50\\end{align*}$$\nThe horizontal axis is in thousands of dollars, so the graph's gradient is $50$ dollars of interest per $1000$ dollars of principal.\n$$\\begin{align*}k&=\\frac{50}{1000}\\\\k&=0.05\\end{align*}$$\nFor one year, $I=Pr$, so $k$ is the annual interest rate: $5\\%$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
